They are well within, you know, a human lifetime.
Okay.

00:14:51.223 --> 00:14:57.329
We began to find some of these within the 100 million light years that the Cephied variables worked.

00:14:57.730 --> 00:15:02.101
And so we were able use the Cephied variables to calibrate the Type 1a supernova.

00:15:02.101 --> 00:15:03.269
Sure, sure.

00:15:03.269 --> 00:15:08.107
And because Type 1a supernovas are so bright, we can see them out much, much farther.

00:15:08.107 --> 00:15:11.777
We're looking at 3 billion light years.

00:15:11.777 --> 00:15:14.613
And so we have the next rung on the ladder.
Sure, sure.

00:15:14.647 --> 00:15:17.549
[Transition Music]

00:15:17.616 --> 00:15:23.923
Once we're beyond the range of Type 1a supernovas, we have to use redshift.

00:15:23.923 --> 00:15:26.525
It's the only ... I was wondering if that was going to play a part in this.

00:15:26.525 --> 00:15:28.961
It's the only thing that we have left.

00:15:28.961 --> 00:15:34.133
But because some redshift is measurable within the range of Type 1a supernovas,

00:15:34.133 --> 00:15:38.003
we can use the Type 1a supernovas to calibrate the redshift for distance.

00:15:38.003 --> 00:15:40.572
And so now we have our last rung on the ladder.

00:15:41.040 --> 00:15:46.412
In fact, there are some distances within the range where redshift is the only thing that works.

00:15:46.412 --> 00:15:51.350
Where it doesn't even make sense anymore to mention a distance in parsecs or light years.

00:15:51.350 --> 00:15:52.918
Because it's just so far.

00:15:52.918 --> 00:15:57.523
It's so far away that we end up with significant ambiguity

00:15:57.523 --> 00:16:01.026
about what the word distance even means.
That's absurd.

00:16:01.026 --> 00:16:02.695
That doesn't even make sense.

00:16:02.695 --> 00:16:04.997
Not only does it become model dependent.

00:16:04.997 --> 00:16:07.766
But depending on what kind of distance you want.

00:16:07.766 --> 00:16:11.070
Do you want, proper distance, the distance that they currently are?

00:16:11.070 --> 00:16:13.072
Or do you want the distance that they were in the past

00:16:13.072 --> 00:16:15.674
or the distance that the light traveled? They’re all different.

00:16:15.674 --> 00:16:18.377
So at that point, do you just say that it is far, far away?

